==================================================
5. STRUCTURED QUERY PLAN
==================================================

The LLM must not initially return raw MQL.

It must first return a validated Pydantic QueryPlan.

Create models similar to:

class QueryPlan:
    contract_id: str
    contract_version: str
    intent: str
    collection: str
    operation: Literal["find", "aggregate"]
    dimensions: list[str]
    metrics: list[str]
    requested_fields: list[str]
    filters: list[QueryFilter]
    grouping: list[str]
    sorting: list[QuerySort]
    limit: int
    explanation: str
    requires_clarification: bool
    clarification_question: str | None

class QueryFilter:
    business_field: str
    operator: Literal[
        "eq", "ne", "gt", "gte", "lt", "lte",
        "in", "nin", "between", "exists"
    ]
    value: Any

The QueryPlan must use business field names, not physical MongoDB field names.

A deterministic compiler must map business field names to physical paths.

Example:

    totalRebate → rebateAmount
    totalScripts → scriptCount
    channel → channel
    clientName → client.name

==================================================
6. TRUSTED CONTEXT
==================================================

The following values must come only from the authenticated application
context and never from the LLM or user text:

- userId
- tenantId
- permittedClientIds
- role
- permissions
- environment
- dataAccessScopes

Business identifiers such as rptId can come from:

1. selected UI context
2. validated conversation context
3. explicit user input

The application must inject mandatory filters after LLM planning.

Example:

{
  "tenantId": authenticated_user.tenant_id,
  "clientId": {"$in": authenticated_user.permitted_client_ids},
  "rptId": resolved_business_context.rpt_id
}

The LLM must not remove or override mandatory filters.

==================================================
7. MONGODB METADATA PROVIDED TO THE MODEL
==================================================

Provide only metadata for permitted and relevant collections.

For each selected collection, provide:

- annotated schema
- business field descriptions
- physical field paths
- BSON types
- required versus optional fields
- array fields
- nested fields
- two or three representative redacted sample documents
- existing indexes
- estimated collection size
- approved relationships to other collections

Serialize BSON samples using Extended JSON representation.

Truncate:

- long strings
- large nested objects
- large arrays
- embedding arrays
- sensitive values

Do not send complete production documents to the LLM.

Do not expose:

- member identifiers
- credentials
- secrets
- connection strings
- prohibited fields
- unnecessary client data

Cache metadata using collection schema version and index version.

Invalidate the cache when schema metadata or indexes change.

==================================================
8. MQL GENERATION RULES
==================================================

The MQL generator may produce only:

- find queries
- read-only aggregation pipelines

Allowed stages must be controlled by each business contract.

Globally prohibit:

- $out
- $merge
- $where
- $function
- $accumulator
- mapReduce
- server-side JavaScript
- insert
- update
- replace
- delete
- drop
- rename
- createIndex
- any administrative command

Allow $lookup only when:

- explicitly permitted by the business contract
- both collections are allowed
- join fields are approved
- the query has passed performance validation

Require:

- an early $match using mandatory filters
- explicit $project
- explicit limits
- correct BSON types
- explicit handling of null and missing values
- appropriate array operators
- stable sorting when pagination is used

For dates use proper BSON Date values.

For identifiers use ObjectId only when the annotated schema says the field
is an ObjectId.

For financial values preserve Decimal128 precision.

Do not convert financial amounts to floating-point values during business
calculations.

==================================================
9. QUERY VALIDATION
==================================================

Implement two separate validation layers.

Layer 1: Deterministic policy validation

Validate:

- selected contract
- contract version
- collection allowlist
- fields
- metrics
- dimensions
- operators
- aggregation stages
- permissions
- mandatory filters
- result limit
- lookup restrictions
- prohibited operators
- tenant isolation

Layer 2: MongoDB query checker

Use QueryMongoDBCheckerTool or an equivalent isolated checker to validate:

- MQL syntax
- referenced collections
- referenced fields
- BSON data types
- aggregation stage ordering
- grouping logic
- projections
- null handling
- array handling

The checker must never execute a query.

If the checker modifies the query, run deterministic validation again.

==================================================
10. PERFORMANCE PREFLIGHT
==================================================

Before execution:

- use explain with queryPlanner mode where feasible
- verify that mandatory filters can use approved indexes
- reject or request approval for a COLLSCAN on large collections
- reject unbounded sorting
- reject unbounded $lookup
- reject excessive pipeline complexity
- enforce maximum aggregation stages
- enforce maximum result rows
- enforce maxTimeMS
- enforce driver-side timeout
- enforce application request timeout

Use configurable defaults:

- maximum result rows: 100
- absolute maximum result rows: 500
- maxTimeMS: 10,000
- maximum aggregation stages: 15
- maximum lookups: 1
- maximum LLM planning retries: 2

Make these values configurable by contract and environment.

Support routing analytical agent traffic to an approved read replica or
analytics workload node instead of the primary transactional workload.

==================================================
11. QUERY EXECUTION
==================================================

Use a MongoDB account with read-only permissions.

Use:

- connection pooling
- TLS
- secrets from environment variables or a secret manager
- maxTimeMS
- result limits
- correlationId
- userId
- conversationId
- contractId
- contractVersion

Never log:

- passwords
- connection strings
- complete sensitive documents
- LLM provider secrets

Return structured execution metadata:

{
  "collection": "...",
  "operation": "aggregate",
  "rowCount": 10,
  "truncated": false,
  "executionTimeMs": 125,
  "contractId": "...",
  "contractVersion": "...",
  "queryHash": "...",
  "indexUsed": "...",
  "warnings": []
}

==================================================
12. RESULT VALIDATION
==================================================

The result validator must check:

- execution succeeded
- result schema matches the contract
- mandatory output fields are present
- prohibited fields are absent
- sensitive values are masked
- totals are numeric
- financial precision is preserved
- derived metrics use approved formulas
- division-by-zero is handled
- results are not unexpectedly empty
- result truncation is reported
- totals reconcile when reconciliation rules exist

The LLM must never calculate authoritative business totals when those totals
can be calculated deterministically.

Calculate totals and formulas in MongoDB or application code.

The LLM may explain verified totals but must not recreate them.

